A||||9||HummelHund||Yet More Fiero Idle Problems||||5||
Z||000000||HummelHund||04-15-2001||06:17 PM||||Ok, here's the rundown. My '84 SE 2M4 does the horrible idle up and down thing (600-1500rpm over and over again) when it's first started, and usually sputters and dies after about a minute. When you turn it back on it idles normally for a few seconds, then races up to about 2500rpm, where it stays for around 1-2 minutes. It will then work its way back down to a smooth, 1000rpm idle and stay there, running perfectly. What is causing this? I replaced most of the vaccuum hoses around the TBI along with the MAP sensor. The car used to idle horribly all the time until I replaced all that stuff, now it'll run great if you let it run through the paces I mentioned above. <BR>What do I need to do to fix this? It's driving me even crazier than I was before!!!<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wade87GT||04-15-2001||06:39 PM||||Sean,<P>While the engine is running, spray WD-40 around the vacuum lines and connections. If there is a leak it will respond with an increase in RPM. Spray around the intake manifold gaskets and the EGR too. It's cheap troubleshooting, but it may find the a solution.<P>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||04-15-2001||07:10 PM||||Hey Hund!<BR>Did you reset the ECM?<BR>Disconnect the battery and wait a minute then reconnect it...<BR>This restores the factory codes, then, as you drive, it tunes back up...<BR>If it still acts wacky, then there are other leaks.<BR>One leak some people miss is the vac line going to the brake booster chamber...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||HummelHund||04-15-2001||10:18 PM||||I did reset the ECM, I thought that was what was wrong with it at first, but it still idled wierd. I had the battery cable disconnected for a good 2 hours last night while I was replacing my throttle cable, and it still idles badly. I'll try the WD-40 thing to see if I can find the leak. <BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroMaster88||04-16-2001||02:38 AM||||Could also try changing the IAC (idle air control) sensor.  It is located on the side of the TBI, the sensor with the 4 connector harness.  My 84 coupe did that idle thing, changed the IAC and the problem stopped.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">       <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/smiling2.gif">  <BR>84 Indy Fiero (restoration Project)  <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/3dlil/eek2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||04-16-2001||07:51 AM||||Some one had this problem before. thow far feched try charging up the battery overnite and see if it does it in the morning<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||blade56_2001||04-16-2001||08:14 AM||||also check the distributor and the ignition coil, pull the cap off that sucker and take alook inside I did on mine and I was suprised that it ran at all, it was nut'n but rust, the ignition pick up has a white cloth or something around it, well I touched it and it crumbled to dust.||||reg||1
Z||000007||HummelHund||04-16-2001||04:54 PM||||I went out to the Fiero today and WD-40'd every vaccuum hose I could find (all those listed on the decklid diagram). No change in idle, I did wait until it was idling smoothly to do it. I'll try the battery and the IAC to see if that helps. <BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000008||HummelHund||04-16-2001||08:20 PM||||I tested the IAC valve today by pulling the connector off and starting the car. I have read that the car should rev up to about 3000rpm immediatley after you start it. My car did not, it stayed around normal idle speed. I'll be replacing the IAC, the cheapest I've found is a Wells IAC at Autozone for $29.99. Has anyone had problems with using the Wells aftermarket IAC as opposed to the AC Delco, which is something like $65 at gmpartsdirect.com? If only the Fiero gods would help me with my idle problems. Perhaps I should sacrifice an MR2?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||04-16-2001||08:28 PM||||I usually pay ohmage to the Fiero god by sacrificing Miata's <P>Give the new part a shot but usually when they go bad they dont work period.<BR>I've never had a problem with autozone NOW Royal Auto is a different story, O2 sensors 3 of 4 went bad in less than a week finally went elsewhere<BR>just for kicks charge that battery<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-16-2001).]||||reg||10
